# wireplumber -- pipewire's session manager, and the package that decides
# whether the audio stack does anything.
#
# pipewire is a graph and a daemon; it does not, on its own, choose a default
# sink, follow a device being plugged in, restore a stream's volume, or route
# anything anywhere. wireplumber is the policy that does. A system with pipewire
# and no session manager starts cleanly and plays no sound, so this is the last
# load-bearing package of the audio half rather than an add-on to it.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
#
# md5 2cbb662f91da2bdce31fa55bef5dfcf5 is the book's, checked against the same
# bytes as the sha256 below.
SRC_URL=https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/pipewire/wireplumber/-/archive/0.5.10/wireplumber-0.5.10.tar.bz2
SRC_SHA256=c89fa29694e89939c0e57cade5c9a7da8e5da21c19071862978ef5f3a346f1e7
SRC_DIR=wireplumber-0.5.10

# -Dsystem-lua=true IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE REMOVED.
#
# The option defaults to FALSE, and the false branch is not a fallback that
# fires when the system lua is missing -- it is unconditional:
#
#     lua_proj = subproject('lua', default_options: ['default_library=static'])
#
# The tarball's subprojects/ directory contains exactly one file, lua.wrap, and
# no lua source. That wrap is download-only ([wrap-file] with source_url at
# lua.org and patch_url at wrapdb), and _scripts/build-meson.sh passes
# --wrap-mode=nodownload, so the default build cannot succeed here at all.
#
# VERIFIED THE DANGEROUS WAY ROUND rather than assumed: a download-only wrap can
# still fire if the tarball also ships subprojects/packagecache/, which meson
# satisfies from without any network access -- reading the .wrap alone would
# then say "inert" when it is not. Checked against the tarball members, not the
# unpacked tree: there is no packagecache and no vendored archive of any kind.
# So this is the loud case, and the flag is required to build rather than
# required to avoid a silent vendored copy. The copy it would have fetched is
# lua 5.4.4 -- older than the 5.4.8 packaged here, statically linked, and
# invisible to every check in this tree.
#
# -Dsystem-lua-version IS DELIBERATELY LEFT AT 'auto', and this is the
# non-obvious half of the pair. Setting it to '5.4' does NOT widen the search;
# it narrows it to exactly two pkg-config names, lua-5.4 and lua5.4, and calls
# error() if neither exists. Duct's lua ships lua.pc -- the name BLFS gives it
# and the name upstream lua has no opinion about -- which matches NEITHER. The
# 'auto' path tries seven names and reaches plain `lua` on the last one:
#
#     dependency('lua', version: ['>=5.3.0'], required: false)
#
# So pinning the version here, which looks like the more explicit and therefore
# safer choice, is the one that fails. It is stated rather than omitted for
# exactly that reason.
#
# ENABLED, and why each matters rather than what it is:
#   modules, daemon, tools   the session manager itself, and wpctl, which is
#                            what any diagnosis of "no sound" starts with
#   elogind                  wireplumber asks logind whether the session is
#                            active before it will take a device on a seat.
#                            Named rather than left at auto for the reason
#                            below.
#
# DISABLED, with what each one would otherwise do:
#   systemd, systemd-user-service, systemd-system-service
#                            Duct has elogind and no systemd. THE FIRST OF
#                            THESE IS THE TRAP: elogind installs a libsystemd.pc
#                            compatibility symlink, so dependency('libsystemd')
#                            SUCCEEDS here, and -Dsystemd left at 'auto' would
#                            quietly build the systemd integration against
#                            elogind's alias. systemd-user-service additionally
#                            defaults to TRUE, so it installs a user unit into a
#                            systemd directory on a system with no systemd
#                            unless it is turned off by name. Same trap pipewire
#                            handles with -Dlogind-provider=libelogind.
#   introspection            gobject-introspection IS packaged, so 'auto' would
#                            switch this on -- an auto feature is not optional
#                            in a tree that ships the dependency. Nothing here
#                            consumes wireplumber's typelib; enabling it would
#                            add two build dependencies to generate data with no
#                            reader.
#   doc                      doxygen and sphinx, none of them packaged. 'auto'
#                            would already resolve to off, and it is stated so
#                            the artefact does not change if doxygen ever
#                            arrives in the builder image.
#   tests, dbus-tests        both default TRUE. Build time spent on binaries
#                            that are not installed, and dbus-tests wants a
#                            session bus that a build container does not have.
MESON_ARGS="-Dsystem-lua=true -Dsystem-lua-version=auto \
-Dmodules=true -Ddaemon=true -Dtools=true \
-Delogind=enabled \
-Dsystemd=disabled -Dsystemd-user-service=false -Dsystemd-system-service=false \
-Dintrospection=disabled -Ddoc=disabled \
-Dtests=false -Ddbus-tests=false"
